1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,785
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

2
00:00:04,785 --> 00:00:09,052
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

3
00:00:09,433 --> 00:00:13,511
<font color="#7FD4E6">And today, we meet the code that
most quantum hardware is betting on.</font>

4
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:19,443
Every code we've built so far
has distance three, so each one

5
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:22,739
can fix a single error and no
more, and a real machine with

6
00:00:22,739 --> 00:00:25,978
thousands of qubits is going to
need a lot more than that.

7
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:30,669
So here is a question for you. If we
want a code that can fix many errors at

8
00:00:30,669 --> 00:00:34,825
once, do you think its checks will have
to reach across more and more qubits?

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,172
<font color="#7FD4E6">I would think so. More errors to catch,
so each check has to look at more qubits?</font>

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,994
Well, that's what the codes so far would
suggest, since Shor's checks already touch

11
00:00:45,994 --> 00:00:50,379
six qubits. But the surface code can be
made as strong as you like, and every one

12
00:00:50,379 --> 00:00:54,270
of its checks still touches just four
qubits sitting next to each other.

13
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:59,114
Here's how it's laid out. Draw
a square grid, put one qubit

14
00:00:59,114 --> 00:01:02,370
on every edge rather than on
the points, and then glue the

15
00:01:02,370 --> 00:01:05,398
top edge to the bottom and the
left edge to the right.

16
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:09,354
That gluing turns the flat sheet
into the surface of a doughnut,

17
00:01:09,354 --> 00:01:12,741
and it's why this first version
is called the toric code. With L

18
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:16,127
squares in each direction, you
end up with two L squared qubits.

19
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,992
At every point where four edges meet, we
put an X check on those four edges, and

20
00:01:20,992 --> 00:01:25,066
around every little square we put a Z
check on the four edges of its border.

21
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,205
<font color="#7FD4E6">And a point and a
square never clash?</font>

22
00:01:28,533 --> 00:01:32,133
They don't, because a point and
a square either share no edges

23
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:35,379
at all or share two of them,
and two clashes cancel, the

24
00:01:35,378 --> 00:01:38,978
same even overlap we needed for
CSS codes in the last section.

25
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:44,131
In hardware, each check gets an ancilla of
its own placed in the middle of its four

26
00:01:44,131 --> 00:01:48,361
qubits, and four CNOTs to its nearest
neighbours are all the wiring it needs.

27
00:01:48,933 --> 00:01:53,895
Counting is quick. Multiplying every point
check together gives nothing, and so does

28
00:01:53,895 --> 00:01:58,738
multiplying every square check, so two of
the checks aren't independent, and two L

29
00:01:58,738 --> 00:02:03,341
squared qubits minus two L squared minus
two checks leaves two logical qubits.

30
00:02:04,867 --> 00:02:08,893
Now let a few Z errors land on edges
that join up into a path, like this

31
00:02:08,893 --> 00:02:12,352
chain of four, and think about which
point checks will notice.

32
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:15,367
Before I light anything
up, take a guess. Which

33
00:02:15,367 --> 00:02:17,748
checks along this path
do you think will fire?

34
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:21,216
<font color="#7FD4E6">All the ones along
the path, I suppose?</font>

35
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:25,497
Well, that seems natural, since every
point on the path touches an error.

36
00:02:25,497 --> 00:02:29,461
But a point in the middle of the chain
touches two errored edges, and two

37
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:33,206
clashes cancel, so the checks that
fire are just the two at its ends.

38
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,521
So the syndrome never shows you the
chain, it shows you where the chain

39
00:02:37,521 --> 00:02:40,976
stops, and the decoder has to work
backwards from those endpoints.

40
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:45,659
X errors behave the same way on the
shifted grid whose points are the centres

41
00:02:45,659 --> 00:02:49,858
of the squares, so a chain of X errors
lights up two square checks at its ends.

42
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:54,442
And a chain that closes up
into a loop has no ends at

43
00:02:54,442 --> 00:02:57,836
all, so it trips no check, and
that is where the danger is.

44
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:01,908
If the loop just encloses a patch
of the grid, it is the product of

45
00:03:01,908 --> 00:03:05,435
the square checks inside it, so it
did nothing to the encoded state,

46
00:03:05,435 --> 00:03:08,541
much like the innermost region
from section eight point two.

47
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:11,699
<font color="#7FD4E6">And a loop that goes all the
way around the doughnut?</font>

48
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:16,494
A loop around the doughnut is the
dangerous one, because it can't be

49
00:03:16,494 --> 00:03:20,277
written as a product of checks, so
it slips past every test and still

50
00:03:20,277 --> 00:03:23,615
changes the encoded state, which
makes it a logical operator.

51
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,429
The shortest loop around the torus
has L edges, so the distance is L,

52
00:03:28,429 --> 00:03:31,954
and that is the whole trick of
this code: to make it stronger,

53
00:03:31,954 --> 00:03:35,767
you simply make the grid bigger,
and the checks stay the same size.

54
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:39,635
<font color="#7FD4E6">So it really is two
L squared, two, L.</font>

55
00:03:39,967 --> 00:03:43,166
Right. And that last L is the
part I like most, because

56
00:03:43,166 --> 00:03:46,602
whether an error is harmless or
fatal now comes down to one

57
00:03:46,602 --> 00:03:50,215
geometric question, whether its
loop can be shrunk to a point.

58
00:03:51,733 --> 00:03:55,432
So how does the decoder work? It's
handed a set of lit-up points, and

59
00:03:55,432 --> 00:03:58,859
it joins them in pairs with paths
that are as short as possible,

60
00:03:58,859 --> 00:04:01,905
then applies errors along those
paths to cancel them out.

61
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:06,679
If its guess and the real error together
form a loop that can be shrunk away, the

62
00:04:06,679 --> 00:04:10,650
encoded state comes back untouched, even
if the guess took a different route.

63
00:04:11,233 --> 00:04:14,279
It only fails when error and
correction together wrap

64
00:04:14,279 --> 00:04:17,501
around the torus, and that
takes about L over two errors

65
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:20,839
lined up in a row, which gets
rare fast as the grid grows.

66
00:04:21,433 --> 00:04:24,702
Pairing points with the least total
length is a classical matching

67
00:04:24,702 --> 00:04:28,022
problem, and there are algorithms
that solve it in polynomial time.

68
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:32,669
And since nobody builds a doughnut-
shaped chip, in practice the torus

69
00:04:32,669 --> 00:04:36,679
is cut open into a flat patch with
two kinds of boundary, which holds

70
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:40,749
one logical qubit and uses about d
squared data qubits for distance d.

71
00:04:42,267 --> 00:04:45,621
So the surface code turns errors
into chains, sees only their

72
00:04:45,621 --> 00:04:48,863
ends, and fails only when a
chain wraps all the way around,

73
00:04:48,863 --> 00:04:52,329
which you can push as far away
as you like by growing the grid.

74
00:04:52,767 --> 00:04:55,982
<font color="#7FD4E6">But every gate that measures
those checks can go wrong as</font>

75
00:04:55,982 --> 00:04:58,336
<font color="#7FD4E6">well. Does adding more
qubits really help?</font>

76
00:04:58,667 --> 00:05:01,590
Adding qubits does help, but
only if the hardware is good

77
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:04,565
enough to begin with, and in
section eight point six we'll

78
00:05:04,565 --> 00:05:07,436
see where that line sits,
which is called the threshold.
